const e={episodes:{1:{points:[{title:"God Is the Source of Life",body:""},{title:"Life Is Sacred",body:""},{title:"Life Is Given for a Purpose",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`People spend years trying to discover the meaning of life while ignoring the One who gave
them life.

If God is the Author of life...

then He alone defines its purpose.

Before God gave Adam work, a wife, or a garden — He gave him *life*.

Every heartbeat is borrowed from God.`,challenge:`Every morning this week, pray:

*"Lord, thank You for the gift of today. Help me live this day for Your glory. May every
breath, every decision, and every opportunity bring honor to You."*`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You for the gift of life.

Thank You for every breath You have placed within me.

Help me never to waste the life You have given me.

May every day bring glory to Your Name.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"You are not an accident, and you are not self-made — life begins with the breath of God.",transition:"",questions:["Who gave me life?","Am I living for the One who gave it?","How can I honor God with the life He has entrusted to me?"],apply:["Thank God *specifically* for the gift of today — name it out loud.","Live this day as borrowed time entrusted to you, not owned by you.",'Ask one question before every major decision today: *"Does this honor the One who gave'],talk:"What would change if you treated today as a gift you have to give an account for?"},2:{points:[{title:"God Delights in Blessing His Children",body:""},{title:"Long Life Allows Greater Fruitfulness",body:""},{title:"Long Life Lets Us Influence More Generations",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Many people fear growing old.

But Scripture treats long life as something God *gives*, not merely something that
*happens*.

If long life is a blessing from God, then the years ahead are not a burden to dread —
they are an inheritance to steward.`,challenge:`Every morning this week, pray:

*"Lord, thank You for the gift of today. Help me live this day for Your glory. May every
breath, every decision, and every opportunity bring honor to You."*`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that long life is Your blessing, not my burden.

Satisfy my days with Your presence and Your purpose.

Make me fruitful in every season You give me.

Let my years bless the generations that come after me.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Long life is not merely the absence of death — it is one of God's covenant blessings.",transition:"",questions:["Do I see my years as a blessing or a burden?","What fruit could my life produce if I steward my years for God?"],apply:["Thank God for the years you have *already* been given.","Name one way your current season could bless someone younger than you.","Stop viewing aging as decline; start viewing added years as added opportunity."],talk:"What's the difference between a long life and a satisfying one?"},3:{points:[{title:"Every Life Has an Assignment",body:""},{title:"God Numbers Our Days",body:""},{title:"The Goal Is to Finish Well",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`God didn't promise to fill your *calendar*.

He promised to fulfill your *days*.

There is a difference between merely passing time and completing a God-given course.

Long life is not the goal. **Finishing well** is the goal.`,challenge:`Every morning this week, pray:

*"Lord, thank You for the gift of today. Help me live this day for Your glory. May every
breath, every decision, and every opportunity bring honor to You."*`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that my days are numbered by You and held in Your hand.

Teach me to number them, that I may live with wisdom.

Keep me faithful to the assignment You have given me.

Help me not only to live long, but to finish well — still carrying the faith.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"God preserves us not merely so we can live longer, but so we can finish our assignment.",transition:"",questions:["What has God assigned me that I have not yet finished?","Am I living to pass time, or to fulfill my days?"],apply:['Name the "baton" God has put in your hands — the assignment you must not drop.',"Refuse the lie that your useful years are behind you.","Take one concrete step today that moves your God-given assignment forward."],talk:"What is the one assignment you don't want to leave unfinished?"},4:{points:[{title:"The Spirit Is Fed by the Word of God",body:""},{title:"The Spirit Is Fed by Prayer",body:""},{title:"The Spirit Is Fed by Worship",body:""},{title:"A Starved Spirit Cannot Sustain a Long Life",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`A weak body can still serve God.

A weak mind can still be renewed.

But a starved spirit cannot sustain a long, fruitful life.

You cannot pour out for seventy years from a well you never fill.

The secret to lasting isn't found in lasting harder — it's found in feeding deeper.`,challenge:"This week, set aside 15 minutes each morning to feed your spirit — open the Word, pray, and worship before you touch your phone.",prayer:`Father,

I confess I have fed my body and starved my spirit.

Forgive me, and teach me to hunger for You.

Feed me with Your Word.

Draw me into prayer.

Lift my heart into worship.

Keep my spirit strong all the days of my life,

that I may serve You to the very end.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"What you feed grows, and what you starve dies — so feed your spirit daily on the Word, prayer, and worship.",transition:"",questions:["What have I been feeding most — my body, my mind, or my spirit?","When did I last sit with God's Word and let it actually feed me?","What one daily habit could keep my spirit fed for the long haul?"],apply:["Feed your spirit *first* — before your phone, before the news, before email.","Read until one verse feeds you, then pray it back to God.","Give five minutes to worship before your tasks begin.",'Tonight, ask: *"Did I feed my spirit today, or only my body?"*'],talk:"Be honest — what have you been feeding most this week, and what has it cost your spirit?"},5:{points:[{title:"A Walk Means Daily, Consistent Fellowship",body:""},{title:"A Walk Means a Life That Pleases God",body:""},{title:"A Walk Means Obedience in an Ungodly World",body:""},{title:"A Walk Stretches Across a Lifetime",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`You will not coast into closeness with God.

Nobody drifts into intimacy.

The believers who last decades are not the ones who had one mountaintop experience. They are the ones who showed up, day after ordinary day, and walked.

A relationship measured in years is built in steps you take today.`,challenge:"This week, set aside 15 minutes each morning to feed your spirit — open the Word, pray, and worship before you touch your phone.",prayer:`Father,

I want to walk with You — not just visit You.

Teach me to take a step toward You every single day.

Keep me beside You in an ungodly world.

Let my life please You all my days.

And like Enoch, may I walk so closely with You

that I am never far from home.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Longevity isn't built in a single moment with God — it's built in a daily walk with Him.",transition:"",questions:["Am I walking *with* God daily, or only visiting Him occasionally?","Would my life carry Enoch's testimony — that it pleased God?","Where is the crowd walking one way while God walks another?"],apply:["Set a fixed daily appointment with God and treat it as unmissable.","Take today's step: read a little, pray a little, obey what you hear.",'Before each decision, ask: *"Does this keep me walking beside God?"*',"Walk God's direction in one area, even if you walk it alone."],talk:"Is your faith a finished decision or a continuing walk — and when did you last take a step?"},6:{points:[{title:"God Wants Your Whole Heart, Not Part of It",body:""},{title:"Relationship Comes Before Religion",body:""},{title:"Love Is the Root of a Long, Satisfied Life",body:""},{title:"We Love Because He First Loved Us",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`You can do everything right and still miss the one thing.

You can attend, serve, give, and recite — and never love.

God is not first looking for your performance. He is looking for your heart.

Religion can be sustained for a while on willpower. But only love lasts a lifetime — and only love makes that lifetime satisfying.`,challenge:"This week, set aside 15 minutes each morning to feed your spirit — open the Word, pray, and worship before you touch your phone.",prayer:`Father,

I don't want religion without relationship.

I don't want to serve You with cold hands and a far-off heart.

Forgive me for the ways I have performed without love.

Help me to love You with all my heart, all my soul, all my might.

You first loved me — let me love You in return,

all the days of my long life.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Long, satisfied life is rooted not in religious activity but in wholehearted love for God.",transition:"",questions:["Do I love God, or do I only serve Him out of habit?","What is competing for the first place in my heart?","When did I last simply enjoy God, rather than work for Him?"],apply:["Tell God you love Him — out loud, before you do anything for Him.","Name and surrender the rival competing for your heart's first place.",'Turn one religious "task" today back into a relationship.',"Sit a few minutes in His love for you at the cross, and let yours rise in answer."],talk:"Is your faith mostly tasks or mostly love — and which one is wearing you out?"},7:{points:[{title:"Faith Believes God Before It Sees the Result",body:""},{title:"Faith Holds On Through the Long Delay",body:""},{title:"Faith Refuses to Draw Back",body:""},{title:"Faith Stays Strong to the Very End",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Anyone can believe for a moment.

The crowd believed at the Red Sea and complained three days later.

The question is not whether you can start by faith. The question is whether your faith can *last* — through delay, through wilderness, through forty silent years.

A long life for God is impossible without a faith that endures. "The just shall live by faith" — not flare and fade, but *live*.`,challenge:"This week, set aside 15 minutes each morning to feed your spirit — open the Word, pray, and worship before you touch your phone.",prayer:`Father,

Give me faith that does not quit.

When I cannot see, help me trust Your Word.

When the wait is long, keep me believing.

When giants stand in the way, make me bold like Caleb.

Let me not draw back, but live by faith —

all the way to the finish line.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Faith is not a single leap — it is the endurance that carries you across decades.",transition:"",questions:["Where am I living by sight when God is calling me to live by faith?","What delay is tempting me to draw back?","Is my faith built like a firework or like a marathon?"],apply:["Take the first step on one thing God told you to do, even without proof.","Name the promise you're waiting on, and choose to keep believing through the delay.",`Draw a line where you're tempted to quit: *"I will not draw back."*`,'Ask God for a "mountain" in this season — and step toward it.'],talk:"Is your faith built like a firework or like a marathon — and what's tempting you to stop?"},8:{points:[{title:"Childhood Has an Assignment",body:""},{title:"Youth Has an Assignment",body:""},{title:"Adulthood Has an Assignment",body:""},{title:"Old Age Has an Assignment",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`The enemy steals seasons by convincing you they don't count.

"You're too young to matter."

"You're too old to be used."

"This in-between season is just waiting."

All lies.

If God has given you breath in this season, He has given you an assignment in it. Longevity for God means serving Him faithfully through every chapter — not just the convenient ones.`,challenge:"This week, set aside 15 minutes each morning to feed your spirit — open the Word, pray, and worship before you touch your phone.",prayer:`Father,

Thank You that no season of my life is wasted.

Forgive me for the years I treated as waiting rooms.

Show me my assignment in the season I am in today.

Whether young or old, busy or slow,

let me bring forth fruit for You —

flourishing all the way to the end.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Every season of life carries a God-given assignment — there is no wasted decade.",transition:"",questions:["What season of life am I in — and have I accepted its assignment?","Have I believed the lie that I'm too young, too old, or just waiting?","Whom in another season could I serve or encourage this week?"],apply:['Name the season you are in right now and ask: *"What is my God-given assignment here?"*','Stop waiting for a "real" season — serve God in this one.',"Offer one current responsibility to God as worship today.","Bless someone in a different season than yours — younger or older."],talk:"Have you been treating your current season as a waiting room — and what assignment might you be missing?"},9:{points:[{title:"The Mind Must Be Renewed, Not Just Restrained",body:""},{title:"The Mind Set on Life Produces Peace",body:""},{title:"Reject Fear — It Was Not Given by God",body:""},{title:"Think According to God's Word",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Your thoughts today become your words tomorrow.

Your words become your actions.

Your actions become your habits — and your habits become your life.

Fear, worry, and bitterness do not just trouble the soul; they wear down the body and rob
the years. A mind set on death cannot produce a life of peace. But a mind renewed by God's
Word can carry you, strong and steady, into every decade He has given you.`,challenge:`This week, catch one fearful or negative thought each day, and replace it out loud with
what God's Word says instead.`,prayer:`Father,

You see every thought before I think it.

Renew my mind by Your Word.

Tear down the fear I have rehearsed for years, and teach me to think life.

Set my mind on what is true, and let Your peace guard my heart.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`A long, fruitful life is not built on what happens to you, but on the thoughts you allow
to live in your mind.`,transition:"",questions:["What is the most common thought I rehearse when no one is watching?","Is my mind set on life and peace, or on fear and death?","What one verse could I carry into every hard moment this week?"],apply:["Catch the very next fearful or negative thought — and say it out loud so you can hear it.","Answer it out loud with one verse of Scripture (start with 2 Timothy 1:7).","Choose one true, lovely, good-report thing from Philippians 4:8 and dwell on it on"],talk:"What thought have you rehearsed so long it has carved a channel in your mind — and what verse will you dig a new channel with?"},10:{points:[{title:"Words Carry Real Power",body:""},{title:"You Will Eat the Fruit of Your Words",body:""},{title:"Speak Faith, Not Fear",body:""},{title:"Speak Life Over Others",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`You have been talking your whole life — but have you ever stopped to weigh what you've been
saying?

Many people pray for a blessed future while speaking defeat over their present. They ask
God for hope at church and rehearse despair at home.

Words set direction. The same tongue that can bless God can curse a brother; the same mouth
that can speak faith can plant fear. If you want to walk in life through every season, you
must learn to *speak* life — over yourself, your family, and your future.`,challenge:`This week, catch one fearful or negative thought each day, and replace it out loud with
what God's Word says instead.`,prayer:`Father,

Set a watch over my mouth, and keep the door of my lips.

Take the power You placed in my tongue and turn it toward life.

Let my words plant faith, hope, and blessing — over myself, my family, and everyone I meet.

Let the words of my mouth be acceptable in Your sight.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`Your words are seeds, and the life you live tomorrow is the harvest of what your mouth
plants today.`,transition:"",questions:["If my words became my future, what kind of life am I building?","Whose voice in my past spoke life or death over me — and which do I now repeat?","What is one sentence I keep saying that I need to stop planting?"],apply:["Catch one negative or fearful word before it leaves your mouth — and replace it out loud","Speak one specific blessing over yourself this morning from Scripture.","Say one true word of life over another person before the day ends."],talk:"What is one sentence you keep speaking that you need to stop planting — and what will you plant instead?"},11:{points:[{title:"Joy Is a Medicine for the Whole Person",body:""},{title:"The Joy of the Lord Is Your Strength",body:""},{title:"Gratitude Changes the Picture",body:""},{title:"Hope Fuels Endurance",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Hard seasons come to everyone. Bad news comes to everyone. The question is not whether
trouble will visit, but what it will find living in you when it arrives.

A broken spirit, Solomon says, *dries the bones* — it drains the strength right out of a
person. But a merry heart works like medicine, day after day, quietly healing and steadying
the one who carries it.

You will not always control your circumstances. But by God's grace, you can choose your
joy — and that choice may carry you further than you know.`,challenge:`This week, catch one fearful or negative thought each day, and replace it out loud with
what God's Word says instead.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that joy in You does not depend on an easy day.

Heal my heart and steady my spirit.

When trouble comes, let me sing like Paul in the prison.

Fill me with all joy and peace in believing, that I may abound in hope.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`Joy is not a reward for an easy life — it is a daily choice that strengthens you to live a
long one.`,transition:"",questions:["Is my heart functioning as a medicine, or is a broken spirit drying my bones?","Where am I waiting for joy *after* the trouble lifts, when God offers it *in* the trouble?","What three mercies of God have I overlooked today?"],apply:["Take your daily dose of joy on purpose — choose it before you feel it.","Out loud, thank God for three specific mercies before you voice a single complaint.","When a fearful or negative thought comes, replace it out loud with a promise of God, and"],talk:"Where in your life are you waiting for joy after the trouble lifts, instead of choosing it in the trouble?"},12:{points:[{title:"The Wise Keep Learning — Fools Think They Know",body:""},{title:"Keep Learning the Word of God Above All",body:""},{title:"Learning Keeps the Mind Sharp and the Life Fruitful",body:""},{title:"The Humble Keep Learning All Their Lives",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`There is a quiet danger that comes with age and experience: we begin to think we already
know.

But the moment you stop learning is the moment you start shrinking. A closed mind grows
stale; a teachable heart stays alive.

God designed you to grow all the way home. Your eyes may dim and your steps may slow, but
your knowledge of God can deepen every single year. The believer who keeps learning keeps
bearing fruit — and a fruitful mind helps make for a fruitful life.`,challenge:`This week, catch one fearful or negative thought each day, and replace it out loud with
what God's Word says instead.`,prayer:`Father,

Keep my heart teachable all the days of my life.

Give me a hunger to know You more this year than I did the last.

Save me from the pride that thinks it has arrived.

Let me, like Paul, be still learning when the end draws near.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`A heart that keeps learning never grows old, because every decade can know more of God
than the one before it.`,transition:"",questions:["Have I quietly decided I already know enough?","Do I know more of God this year than I did last year?","Whom could I humbly let teach me something this week?"],apply:["Ask one honest question today instead of assuming you already know the answer.","Study one verse — don't just read it — and write down one new thing it shows you about God.","Begin learning one worthwhile thing this week, and let someone else teach you part of it."],talk:"Do you know more of God this year than you did last year — and what's your plan to know more next year?"},13:{points:[{title:"Know the Difference: Knowledge, Understanding, Wisdom",body:""},{title:"Wisdom Begins with the Fear of the Lord",body:""},{title:"Ask God for Wisdom — He Gives It Freely",body:""},{title:"Wisdom Multiplies Your Fruitful Years",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`We live in an age drowning in information and starving for wisdom. Never have people known so
much and lived so foolishly.

A renewed mind is not just a *full* mind — it is a *wise* one. Wisdom is the skill of living
life God's way, and it is the crown of everything Week 3 has been building toward: think
life, speak life, choose joy, keep learning — and let it all become wisdom.

Solomon says wisdom holds *length of days* in her right hand. This is where renewing the mind
and a long, fruitful life finally meet.`,challenge:`This week, catch one fearful or negative thought each day, and replace it out loud with
what God's Word says instead.`,prayer:`Father,

You give wisdom liberally to all who ask — so I ask.

Make me wise, not merely informed.

Teach me to fear You, to trust You with all my heart, and to do the right thing with what I
know.

Be a tree of life to me, and let me bear fruit through every season You give.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`Knowledge fills your head, understanding opens your eyes, but wisdom directs your steps —
and wisdom multiplies your fruitful years.`,transition:"",questions:["Am I gathering knowledge but failing to live wisely?","Where am I leaning on my own understanding instead of trusting the Lord?","What is one foolish path I keep choosing because it's faster?"],apply:["Ask God plainly for wisdom this morning, trusting James 1:5 that He gives it freely.",'In your next decision, lean not on your own understanding — pause and ask, *"Lord, what is',"Take one fearful or negative thought and replace it out loud with what God's Word says,"],talk:"Where in your life do you have the knowledge but still need the wisdom to act on it?"},14:{points:[{title:"Your Body Belongs to God",body:""},{title:"Honor Begins with Reverence, Not Vanity",body:""},{title:"You Were Bought with a Price",body:""},{title:"Stewardship Is Worship, and Worship Is Daily",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`We live in a world that treats the body in two extremes.

Some worship it — chasing vanity, mirrors, and applause.

Others neglect it — abusing it as if it doesn't matter.

But God offers a third way.

The body is not a god to be worshipped, and not a thing to be wasted.

It is a temple to be *honored* — for the glory of the One who lives in it.`,challenge:`This week, honor God with your body in one concrete way each day — a healthier meal, a
walk, earlier sleep, or a fast — as worship, not vanity.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that by Your Spirit You have chosen to dwell in me.

Forgive me for the times I have neglected or misused the temple You purchased.

Teach me to honor my body — not for vanity, but for Your glory.

Let everything I do with this body bring honor to the One who bought me with a price.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Your body is not your own — it is God's temple, and stewarding it well is an act of worship.",transition:"",questions:["Do I treat my body as my own, or as God's temple?","Is my care for my body driven by worship or by vanity?","What would change if I remembered the price that was paid for me?"],apply:["Thank God that your body is His temple, not your possession.","Identify one way you have been neglecting or idolizing your body — and repent of it.","Offer one concrete act of care today as worship: a glass of water, a walk, an early night.",'Before a choice about food, rest, or habit, ask: *"Does this honor the One who lives in me?"*'],talk:"If you truly believed your body was God's temple, what one habit would you change this week?"},15:{points:[{title:"God Designed Food for Life, Not Just Pleasure",body:""},{title:"Discipline at the Table Is Stewardship",body:""},{title:"Moderation Is God's Wisdom",body:""},{title:"The Heart Behind the Plate",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`The very first test in the Bible was about food.

"Of every tree of the garden thou mayest freely eat: but of the tree of the knowledge of
good and evil, thou shalt not eat" (Genesis 2:16–17).

Eden's first command involved the table.

And it still does.

How we handle appetite reveals whether we are mastering ourselves —

or being mastered.`,challenge:`This week, honor God with your body in one concrete way each day — a healthier meal, a
walk, earlier sleep, or a fast — as worship, not vanity.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You for the gift of food and for Your provision for my body.

Forgive me where appetite has ruled instead of wisdom.

Give me, like Daniel, a purposed heart — to honor You with what I eat.

Make me temperate, thankful, and strong for Your service.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"What you eat is a matter of stewardship, and discipline at the table is an act of worship.",transition:"",questions:["Does my appetite serve me, or do I serve my appetite?","Where do I need the discipline that Daniel showed at the table?","Am I eating for momentary pleasure, or for a long life of service to God?"],apply:["Thank God for your food before you eat — out loud, on purpose.","Choose one meal today and make it a *stewardship* meal: simpler, healthier, enough.",`Stop eating before you're full; practice the discipline of "sufficient."`,'Ask before a craving: *"Am I feeding the temple, or feeding the flesh?"*'],talk:'What would it look like for you to "purpose in your heart" about food this week?'},16:{points:[{title:"Rest Is Woven into Creation",body:""},{title:"Sleep Is God's Gift to His Beloved",body:""},{title:"Rest Is an Act of Trust",body:""},{title:"Even Jesus Rested",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`We wear exhaustion like a badge of honor.

"I'm so busy." "I barely sleep." "I'll rest when I'm dead."

But a body that never rests breaks down.

A spirit that never stops cannot hear God.

To refuse rest is, quietly, to say: *"It all depends on me."*

Rest says the opposite.

Rest says: *"God is still working while I sleep."*`,challenge:`This week, honor God with your body in one concrete way each day — a healthier meal, a
walk, earlier sleep, or a fast — as worship, not vanity.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that You never slumber, so that I can rest.

Forgive me for living as if everything depends on me.

Teach me to receive sleep as Your gift and rest as an act of trust.

Restore my strength, like Elijah's, that I may serve You for many years.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Rest is not laziness — it is trust, and God built it into the rhythm of a long life.",transition:"",questions:["Do I treat rest as laziness or as trust?","What am I trying to control by refusing to stop?","How would my week change if I honored God's rhythm of work and rest?"],apply:["Set a bedtime tonight and keep it — receive sleep as God's gift.","Hand your worries to God in prayer before you close your eyes.","Plan one true day of rest this week to stop, worship, and recover.",'Ask: *"Am I living as if it all depends on me, or trusting the God who never sleeps?"*'],talk:"What are you trying to control by refusing to rest?"},17:{points:[{title:"Work and Movement Were God's Original Design",body:""},{title:"The Body Grows Strong Through Use",body:""},{title:"Bodily Exercise Profits — In Proportion",body:""},{title:"Diligence Honors God; Slothfulness Wastes the Temple",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Our world has made stillness easy.

Screens. Chairs. Cars. Convenience at every turn.

We can now go days barely moving our bodies at all.

But a body built for the garden does not thrive in a chair.

Strength fades when it is never used.

And a weakened body cannot serve God, family, and neighbor the way it was made to.

Movement is not vanity. It is readiness — keeping the temple fit for its purpose.`,challenge:`This week, honor God with your body in one concrete way each day — a healthier meal, a
walk, earlier sleep, or a fast — as worship, not vanity.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You for a body designed to move, to work, and to serve.

Forgive me where laziness has let Your gift grow weak.

Help me keep this temple strong and ready — like Caleb, willing to climb mountains for You.

And keep my heart in proportion: my body fit, but my soul fed first.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"God designed your body to move and work, and keeping it active is part of stewarding it for His service.",transition:"",questions:["Am I keeping my body ready for the work God has for me?","Have I let convenience make me weak?","Is my care for my body in right proportion to my care for my soul?"],apply:["Take a walk today — even ten minutes — and use it to pray and thank God for your body.","Choose the active option once: the stairs, the longer route, the standing task.",'Do one honest piece of physical work as worship, "as to the Lord" (Colossians 3:23).',"Keep it in proportion: care for the body, but feed your soul first."],talk:"What's one way you could weave more movement into your ordinary day this week?"},18:{points:[{title:"God Calls Us to Cleanse Body and Spirit",body:""},{title:"Temperance Is the Fruit of the Spirit",body:""},{title:"Flee What Defiles",body:""},{title:"Fasting Cleanses and Refocuses",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`The things that shorten a life are rarely a single dramatic event.

They are habits.

What we keep putting in. What we refuse to put down.

The slow poisons — of the body and of the soul — that we tolerate because they feel small.

But God calls His temple to be clean.

Not perfect by our striving —

but cleansed, guarded, and kept pure for His use.`,challenge:`This week, honor God with your body in one concrete way each day — a healthier meal, a
walk, earlier sleep, or a fast — as worship, not vanity.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that what is defiled can be cleansed by Your grace.

Search me, and show me the filthiness of flesh and spirit I have tolerated.

Wash me, and grow in me the fruit of temperance by Your Spirit.

Give me, like Joseph, the courage to flee what defiles and to keep Your temple holy.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"Stewarding the body means keeping it clean — in purity, temperance, and self-control — for the glory of God.",transition:"",questions:[`What small "poisons" have I been tolerating in God's temple?`,"Where do I need to flee instead of negotiate?","Am I trusting willpower, or the Spirit, to make me temperate?"],apply:['Ask God to reveal one "filthiness of flesh or spirit" — and confess it to Him today.',"Flee one thing that defiles you; remove it rather than manage it.","Plan a fast this week — one meal or one day — to humble the body and seek God.","Pray for the Spirit's fruit of temperance over your strongest craving."],talk:`What is one "small poison" you've tolerated that God may be asking you to cleanse?`},19:{points:[{title:"God Gives Strength for the Day, Not the Decade",body:""},{title:"Faithfulness Is a Daily Word, Not a Lifetime Word",body:""},{title:"Worry Steals from the Life God Gave You",body:""},{title:"Today Is the Only Day You Can Honor God In",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`We worry about a future we cannot live in yet.

We carry tomorrow's burden on today's shoulders — and break under weight God never asked
us to lift.

God does not give grace for an imagined tomorrow.

He gives grace for the day you are actually in.

If you want to finish a long race, stop trying to run all the miles at once. **Run the
mile you are on.**`,challenge:`This week, take one deliberate step that outlives you — mentor someone, write down your
testimony, or invest in the next generation.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You for the gift of this single day.

I will not borrow tomorrow's troubles, and I will not waste today's strength on them.

Give me grace for today. Give me bread for today. Give me obedience for today.

As my days are, so let my strength be — fresh from Your hand each morning.

Teach me to build a faithful life one ordinary day at a time, until the last day is done.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"A faithful life is not built in great leaps; it is built one obedient day at a time.",transition:"",questions:["Am I carrying tomorrow's weight on today's strength?","If I am only ever asked to be faithful *today*, what does faithfulness look like in the"],apply:["Name one thing God is asking of you *today* — and do that one thing.","The next time you feel anxious, locate the worry: is it a *today* problem or a",'End the day with a single sentence of thanks: *"Lord, You gave me strength for today."*'],talk:"What tomorrow are you trying to live in that God hasn't given you yet?"},20:{points:[{title:"Spiritual Multiplication Is God's Strategy",body:""},{title:"Invest in the Next Generation on Purpose",body:""},{title:"Leave Spiritual Sons and Daughters, Not Just Heirs",body:""},{title:"Multiplication Costs You — and That's the Point",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Most of what you own will be sold, spent, or thrown away within a generation.

But what you *deposit* in a person can travel through centuries.

Jesus did not write a book. He did not build a building. He invested His three years in
twelve men — and that investment is still spreading across the earth today.

If you want a life that outlives you, stop trying to be irreplaceable. Start trying to be
*reproducible.*`,challenge:`This week, take one deliberate step that outlives you — mentor someone, write down your
testimony, or invest in the next generation.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You for everyone who poured into me so the faith could reach my generation.

I do not want my life to end when my body does.

Show me my Timothy. Show me the next generation You want me to invest in.

Make me a spiritual father, a spiritual mother — leaving sons and daughters in the faith.

Let what You have placed in me multiply through faithful people, long after I am gone, to
the glory of Jesus Christ.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`You will not live forever in your body, so pour your life into people who will carry it
after you.`,transition:"",questions:["When I am gone, what will keep going because I poured it into a person?","Am I trying to be irreplaceable, or am I making myself reproducible?"],apply:["Name one person you could pour into — and reach out to them this week. Don't wait to feel","Write down three things God has taught you that you want to make sure get passed on.",'Pray for a "Timothy" by name — ask God to show you who to invest your life in.'],talk:"Who is your Timothy — and what's stopping you from pouring into them?"},21:{points:[{title:"Righteousness, Not Age, Determines Fruitfulness",body:""},{title:"Abiding Is the Source of Fruit",body:""},{title:"The Fruit of Age Is Often the Sweetest",body:""},{title:"Increasing Influence, Not Ending Influence",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`Many believers quietly retire from usefulness long before they retire from breathing.

They assume their fruitful years are behind them.

But Psalm 92 dismantles that assumption completely: the righteous *"shall still bring forth
fruit in old age."*

Not *some* fruit. Not *leftover* fruit. Fruit so abundant the psalmist calls them "fat and
flourishing."

If you belong to God, your most fruitful season may not be behind you. It may be ahead.`,challenge:`This week, take one deliberate step that outlives you — mentor someone, write down your
testimony, or invest in the next generation.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that in Your kingdom, age is not the end of fruitfulness but the deepening of it.

Plant me firmly in Your house, that my roots may go down and my fruit may keep coming.

Keep me abiding in Christ the Vine, so that even in old age I bring forth fruit.

Let my last decades be my most fruitful — fat and flourishing, full of Your glory.

Use my years, my wisdom, and my testimony to bless the generation coming after me.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:"In God's economy, growing older is meant to increase your fruit, not end it.",transition:"",questions:["Have I quietly retired from usefulness while I still have breath?","What fruit is God still wanting to grow through me in this season of my life?"],apply:['Reject the lie that your fruitful years are over — say out loud, *"I shall still bring',"Tend your roots today: spend unhurried time abiding in Christ, the source of all fruit.","Identify one piece of fruit only your years have grown — and give it away to someone this"],talk:"What fruit could God still grow through you that only your years could produce?"},22:{points:[{title:"Finish with Faith — Keep Believing to the End",body:""},{title:"Finish with Strength — Don't Coast to the Line",body:""},{title:"Finish with Joy — Count It a Privilege, Not a Burden",body:""},{title:"Finish with Confidence — Know Whose Hand Holds You",body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`The Bible is honest: many start well and fail to finish.

Saul began as a humble king and ended consulting a witch. Demas walked with Paul, then
"forsook me, having loved this present world" (2 Timothy 4:10).

Starting is no guarantee of finishing.

But Scripture also shows us finishers — people who crossed the line with the baton still
in hand, still believing, still rejoicing. They were not superhuman. They simply refused
to quit.

You are still running. The line is still ahead. **How you finish is still being decided.**`,challenge:`This week, take one deliberate step that outlives you — mentor someone, write down your
testimony, or invest in the next generation.`,prayer:`Father,

Thank You that You did not call me only to start, but to finish.

When my legs are heavy and the crowd has thinned, hold me up.

Let me finish with faith — still believing.

Let me finish with strength — still climbing mountains for You.

Let me finish with joy — my eyes on the crown and on the face of Jesus.

Let me finish with confidence — resting in Your faithfulness, not my own.

Keep me, that I may one day say with Paul: *I have fought a good fight, I have finished my
course, I have kept the faith.*

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`God's goal for your life is not merely a long race, but a finished one — run with faith,
joy, and confidence to the very end.`,transition:"",questions:["Am I running to coast across the line, or to finish strong?","Is my faith something I am keeping all the way to the end, or quietly setting down?"],apply:["Take inventory: where are you tempted to slow down, coast, or quit? Name it honestly.",'Ask God for a fresh "mountain" — a new assignment for this season, not just a rest from','Fix one image in your mind for the hard miles ahead: the crown, the "well done," the face'],talk:"Where in your walk are you tempted to coast — and what would finishing strong look like there?"},23:{points:[{title:"God's Promise Is Satisfaction, Not Just Length",body:""},{title:"The End Goal Is Seeing His Salvation",body:""},{title:"Looking Back Without Regret",body:""},{title:'Hearing "Well Done, Good and Faithful Servant"',body:""}],supporting:[],illustration:`A person can live a hundred years and reach the end empty.

And a person can be deeply satisfied — full, complete, at peace — because they spent their
years on the only thing that lasts.

God's promise in Psalm 91:16 has two halves, and we must not separate them.

He does not merely promise *length* of days. He promises to *satisfy.* And He defines that
satisfaction by what comes next: *"and shew him my salvation."*

Long life without salvation is just a long road to an empty end. But a life given to God is
a life that arrives **full.**`,challenge:`This week, take one deliberate step that outlives you — mentor someone, write down your
testimony, or invest in the next generation.`,prayer:`Father,

You have walked with me through this whole journey — Foundation, Faith, Focus, Fitness, and
now Finishing.

Thank You that Your promise was never merely a longer life, but a *satisfied* one.

I do not want to reach the end empty. I want to reach it full — having seen Your salvation
in Jesus Christ.

Let me look back one day without regret, knowing my years were spent on what truly matters.

And grant me, by Your grace, to hear the only words I long to hear: *"Well done, thou good
and faithful servant; enter thou into the joy of thy lord."*

With long life satisfy me, O Lord — and show me Your salvation.

In Jesus' Name,

Amen.`,declaration:`The true secret of longevity is not merely to live long, but to reach the end satisfied —
with no regret, seeing God's salvation, and hearing "Well done."`,transition:"",questions:["If my life ended today, would I be satisfied — having seen God's salvation?","What am I spending my years on that I might one day regret? What would I never regret?",'Am I living for the applause of people, or for the "Well done" of God?'],apply:["Make sure of your salvation in Jesus Christ — the one thing the whole series has been","Live this day so that, looking back from the end, you would have no regret about it.",'Order one decision today by the only verdict that lasts: *Would this earn a "Well done"?*'],talk:"If your life ended today, would you arrive satisfied — having seen God's salvation?"}},sundays:{},shorts:[{scripture:'"The LORD God... breathed into his nostrils the breath of life." — Genesis 2:7',truth:"Before you ever took your first breath, God breathed life into humanity. Your greatest possession isn't your money. It's today. Don't waste the day God gave you.",challenge:`Today, don't ask, "How much time do I have?" Ask, "Lord, how do You want me to use today?"`,quote:"Every heartbeat is borrowed from God."},{scripture:'"With long life will I satisfy him, and shew him my salvation." — Psalm 91:16',truth:"God doesn't simply promise a long life. He promises a satisfying one. A long life without purpose is empty. A short life with God has eternal value. But God's desire is both — a long life that glorifies Him.",challenge:"Thank God today for the years He's already given — then ask Him to make them fruitful.",quote:"God doesn't just promise a long life. He promises a satisfying one."},{scripture:'"...the number of thy days I will fulfil." — Exodus 23:26',truth:"God didn't promise to fill your calendar. He promised to fulfill your days. There's a difference. One is busyness. The other is a finished assignment.",challenge:"Live today like someone who still has an assignment — because you do.",quote:"God didn't promise to fill your calendar. He promised to fulfill your days."},{scripture:'"Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of God." — Matthew 4:4',truth:"What you feed grows. What you starve dies. You feed your body three times a day without thinking. When did you last feed the part of you that lives forever? The Word. Prayer. Worship. That's the menu.",challenge:"Tomorrow morning, feed your spirit before you touch your phone. Fifteen minutes. Word, prayer, worship — first.",quote:"What you feed grows. Feed your spirit daily."},{scripture:'"And Enoch walked with God: and he was not; for God took him." — Genesis 5:24',truth:"Enoch walked with God for three hundred years. Not one mountaintop moment — a daily walk. Nobody drifts into closeness with God. You step into it. One ordinary day at a time.",challenge:"Take one step with God today. Read a little, pray a little, obey what you hear. Then do it again tomorrow.",quote:"Nobody drifts into closeness with God. You walk into it — one day at a time."},{scripture:'"Because he hath set his love upon me, therefore will I deliver him." — Psalm 91:14',truth:"Not a ritual. Not a formula. He loved God. The long, satisfied life of Psalm 91 grows from one root — wholehearted love. You can do everything right and still miss the one thing. God wants your heart before your hands.",challenge:"Before you do one thing for God today, tell Him you love Him. Out loud. Relationship before religion.",quote:"God wants your heart before your hands."},{scripture:'"Now the just shall live by faith: but if any man draw back, my soul shall have no pleasure in him." — Hebrews 10:38',truth:"Anyone can believe for a moment. The crowd believed at the Red Sea and complained three days later. Faith isn't a single leap — it's the endurance that carries you across decades. Don't draw back. Keep climbing.",challenge:'Name the place you want to quit. Draw a line today: "I will not draw back." Then take one step forward.',quote:"Faith is not a single leap. It's the endurance that carries you across decades."},{scripture:'"They shall still bring forth fruit in old age; they shall be fat and flourishing." — Psalm 92:14',truth:"Samuel served as a child. David faced Goliath as a youth. Anna worshipped into her eighties. There are no spectator seasons in God's kingdom. Every decade has a divine assignment — and not one of them is wasted.",challenge:`Name the season you're in. Then ask God one question: "What's my assignment here?" Don't wait for a better season. Serve in this one.`,quote:"In God's kingdom there are no wasted decades."},{scripture:'"Be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind." — Romans 12:2',truth:"Ten spies saw giants and thought death. Caleb saw the same giants and thought life — and at eighty-five he was still strong. Same facts. Different minds. Your thoughts today become your life tomorrow.",challenge:"Catch your next fearful thought. Say it out loud — then answer it out loud with what God says.",quote:"Where you fix your mind, there you will live."},{scripture:'"Death and life are in the power of the tongue." — Proverbs 18:21',truth:"God built the world with words. Ten spies spoke death and died in the wilderness. Caleb spoke life and walked into the promise. Your words are seeds — you'll eat the harvest of whatever your mouth plants.",challenge:"Catch your next negative word. Stop it — and speak a promise of God out loud instead.",quote:"If you don't like your harvest, change the seed in your mouth."},{scripture:'"A merry heart doeth good like a medicine." — Proverbs 17:22',truth:"Joy isn't a reward for an easy life — it's a choice that strengthens you to live a long one. Paul and Silas sang at midnight, in chains, and the prison shook. Don't wait for the chains to fall. Choose joy inside them.",challenge:"Before you voice one complaint today, name three things God has done for you — out loud.",quote:"The merry heart is the medicine you choose for yourself."},{scripture:'"A wise man will hear, and will increase learning." — Proverbs 1:5',truth:"Moses was still learning from God at eighty. Daniel got fresh revelation in his eighties. Paul asked for his books from death row. The wise never graduate. Every decade should know more of God than the last.",challenge:"Study one verse today — don't just read it. Write down one new thing it shows you about God.",quote:"A mind that stops learning becomes a fence post. A mind that keeps learning stays a tree."},{scripture:'"Length of days is in her right hand." — Proverbs 3:16',truth:"Knowledge informs you. Understanding explains things. But wisdom applies them — it gets you across the river alive. Solomon asked God for wisdom, and length of days came along in her right hand. And God gives wisdom to anyone who asks.",challenge:'Before your next decision, stop and ask God plainly: "Lord, what is the wise thing to do?"',quote:"Knowledge informs. Understanding explains. Wisdom applies."},{scripture:'"Your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost... ye are not your own." — 1 Corinthians 6:19–20',truth:"God doesn't live in a building anymore. By His Spirit, He lives in you. You were bought with the blood of Jesus. So your body isn't yours to ruin or to worship — it's His to honor. Caring for it isn't vanity. It's worship.",challenge:'Today, do one thing for your body as an act of worship — and say, "Lord, this is Yours."',quote:"Your body is not your own — it's God's temple."},{scripture:`"Daniel purposed in his heart that he would not defile himself with the portion of the king's meat." — Daniel 1:8`,truth:"Daniel turned down the king's rich food, asked for vegetables and water, and ended up healthier than everyone else. He served kings for seventy years. His long life started with one decision at the table. What you eat is stewardship — and discipline is worship.",challenge:"At your next meal, eat for the race, not just the moment. Choose enough — and thank God for it.",quote:"Daniel's seventy years of service began with one meal."},{scripture:'"He giveth his beloved sleep." — Psalm 127:2',truth:`God rested on the seventh day. Jesus slept through a storm. When Elijah was worn out, God's first gift was sleep. Refusing rest says, "It all depends on me." Rest says, "God is still working while I sleep." Rest isn't laziness — it's trust.`,challenge:"Tonight, go to bed earlier. Hand your worries to God, and let your rest be an act of faith.",quote:"Rest is the body's confession that God is in control."},{scripture:'"The LORD God... put the man into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it." — Genesis 2:15',truth:"God's first gift to man, after breath, was work — a body made to move. A hinge that's never used rusts shut. Movement keeps the temple strong and ready to serve. Bodily exercise profits — kept in its place, under godliness.",challenge:"Take a walk today. Use it to pray, thank God for your body, and keep the hinge swinging.",quote:"You were not built for the chair — you were built for the garden."},{scripture:'"Let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit." — 2 Corinthians 7:1',truth:"When Joseph was tempted, he didn't negotiate — he fled, leaving his coat behind. He kept God's temple clean and lived a long, fruitful life. Temperance isn't your willpower; it's the fruit of the Spirit. And what's defiled can be cleansed.",challenge:"Name one thing that defiles you — and flee it today. Then ask the Spirit for self-control.",quote:"Don't negotiate with what defiles you — flee it."},{scripture:'"as thy days, so shall thy strength be." — Deuteronomy 33:25',truth:"God doesn't give you tomorrow's strength today — because you don't need it today. He gives manna fresh every morning. Worry empties today of the strength meant for today. A faithful life is just a long string of obedient days.",challenge:"Hand God every tomorrow-worry. Then go do the one thing He's asking of you today.",quote:"God gives strength by the day — and the day is always enough."},{scripture:'"the same commit thou to faithful men, who shall be able to teach others also." — 2 Timothy 2:2',truth:"You can count the seeds in an apple — but never the apples in a seed. Things you own die with you. People you pour into carry you for generations. Stop trying to be irreplaceable. Start being reproducible.",challenge:"Pick one person this week and pour into them what God poured into you.",quote:"You can count the seeds in an apple, but never the apples in a seed."},{scripture:'"They shall still bring forth fruit in old age; they shall be fat and flourishing." — Psalm 92:14',truth:"The world says fruitfulness has a deadline. God says the righteous flourish like the palm and grow like the cedar — taller, deeper, wider with age. Your last decades are not your least. Stay rooted, keep abiding, keep bearing.",challenge:'Say it out loud today: "I shall still bring forth fruit." Then go give away something only your years could have grown.',quote:"In God's kingdom, your last decades are not your least."},{scripture:'"I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith." — 2 Timothy 4:7',truth:"Anyone can start. The blocks are crowded. Heaven doesn't celebrate the fastest start — it celebrates the finish. Caleb finished with strength. Paul finished with joy. John finished with confidence. The crown is for finishers.",challenge:"Don't coast and don't quit. Ask God for a fresh mountain — and run through the line.",quote:"Heaven doesn't celebrate the fastest start. Heaven celebrates the finish."},{scripture:'"With long life will I satisfy him, and shew him my salvation." — Psalm 91:16',truth:`God's promise was never just more years. It was satisfaction — a life that arrives full. The verse doesn't end with "long life." It ends with "and shew him my salvation." Old Simeon could die in peace, not because he lived long, but because his eyes had seen the Savior.`,challenge:'Make sure of Christ today. Live this day so that at the end you hear, "Well done, good and faithful servant."',quote:"The longest life is the one that ends able to say: 'It was worth every season — and I have seen the Lord.'"}]};export{e as default};
